t worrying about this stuff i  occasionally look it up it says that  that genitive is this way and the  nominative and the feminine and the  masculine stuff and i never retain any  of this stuff  but it's just that i become increasingly  aware as i'm listening whereas the first  time i might only have heard  you know the uh  what was it  i became more aware of that final little  sound that isn't written for me  and and i'm quite comfortable with that  i'm quite comfortable with just letting  the language come into me and at some  point  i will you know on the you know nth time  when i read through this little not so  thick grammar book some of the stuff  will become even clearer because if i  see there that there's eight types of  verbs as it says in this book eight  types of verbs in arabic i am not going  to go through and  i try to understand how these verbs are  different and then try to remember how  they're different it just isn't going to  work  and  in my experience and it reminds me that  there was actually  a person a gentleman who commented  to judge by the the icon here  who commented uh and said  everybody this is  d sean forsyth commenting on one of my  videos here at youtube said everybody  learns by focusing on a thing and  receiving lots of input of all kinds in  mostly random order and the brain sorts  this stuff out beyond our level of  conscious awareness  and then we begin to sort the thing out  ourselves on the conscious level  so this is it we are we are receiving  all this stuff but teachers he says who  learn this way because they're humans  but then they systematize everything and  they give it to students in a systematic  fashion  that is totally unlike how teachers  themselves learn the thing to begin with  so the ordering and systematizing and  rule forming and all that stuff  can only come after  a ton of stuff has actually been  absorbed and this is such an important  point i totally agree  with uh  sean forsyth and it reminds me of  s